# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Habakkuk'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'The Prophet’s Question, the Vision That Tarries, and Joy in the God of Salvation'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Habakkuk is “the burden which Habakkuk the prophet did see” (Hab. 1:1). It opens with the prophet’s complaint, the LORD’s answer that He is raising up the Chaldeans, a second complaint, the vision to be written, five woes, and a prayer that ends in rejoicing though the fig tree does not blossom (Hab. 1:5, 13; 2:2–4; 3:17–19).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the prophet speaks for the LORD’s people and the LORD answers with a vision for an appointed time (Hab. 2:2–3). Its words on faith and on God’s glory are also placed in the Universal Truths Table.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Habakkuk', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Prophet’s Complaint and the LORD’s Answer (Ch. 1)'), ('p', 'The LORD says He will work a work in their days, and the prophet asks how a God of purer eyes can look on the treacherous (Hab. 1:5, 13).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Vision and the Woes (Ch. 2)'), ('p', 'The prophet stands on his watch, is told to write the vision, and hears that the just shall live by his faith (Hab. 2:1–4, 14, 20).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Prayer of Habakkuk (Ch. 3)'), ('p', 'The prophet prays for mercy in wrath and ends with rejoicing in the LORD (Hab. 3:2, 17–19).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Habakkuk', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>A Work in Their Days</b> — “I will work a work in your days, which ye will not believe, though it be told you” (Hab. 1:5).', '<b>Standing on the Watch</b> — “I will stand upon my watch, and set me upon the tower” (Hab. 2:1).', '<b>The Vision Written</b> — “Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables, that he may run that readeth it” (Hab. 2:2).', '<b>The Appointed Time</b> — “The vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie” (Hab. 2:3).', '<b>The Just Shall Live</b> — “The just shall live by his faith” (Hab. 2:4).', '<b>The Earth Filled</b> — “The earth shall be filled with the knowledge of the glory of the LORD, as the waters cover the sea” (Hab. 2:14).', '<b>Joy in the God of Salvation</b> — “Yet I will rejoice in the LORD, I will joy in the God of my salvation” (Hab. 3:18).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Habakkuk', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Habakkuk points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Just Shall Live by His Faith</b> — The words of Habakkuk 2:4 are quoted in the New Testament (Rom. 1:17; Gal. 3:11; Heb. 10:38); each quotation is read in its own letter, and none is read into Habakkuk.', '<b>Though It Tarry</b> — “Though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come” (Hab. 2:3) is reported as written; Hebrews 10:37 has similar words and is read in its own chapter.', '<b>The Earth Filled With Glory</b> — The earth filled with the knowledge of the glory of the LORD (Hab. 2:14) is reported within Israel’s prophetic hope.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Habakkuk', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Habakkuk speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Habakkuk operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Habakkuk in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Habakkuk reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['the LORD’s people for whom the vision is written (Hab. 2:2–3)', 'the chief singer and the prayer of the prophet (Hab. 3:19)', 'the work the LORD works in their days (Hab. 1:5)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Habakkuk that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Habakkuk contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['A Work in Their Days — “I will work a work in your days, which ye will not believe, though it be told you.” (Hab. 1:5).', 'The Vision Written — “Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables.” (Hab. 2:2).', 'The Appointed Time — “the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie.” (Hab. 2:3).', 'The Just Shall Live — “the just shall live by his faith.” (Hab. 2:4).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Habakkuk Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Habakkuk and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Habakkuk teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Habakkuk', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['A Work in Their Days', '“I will work a work in your days, which ye will not believe, though it be told you.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hab. 1:5'], ['The Vision Written', '“Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hab. 2:2'], ['The Appointed Time', '“the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hab. 2:3'], ['The Just Shall Live', '“the just shall live by his faith.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Hab. 2:4']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Habakkuk that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Habakkuk teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Purer Eyes', '“Thou art of purer eyes than to behold evil, and canst not look on iniquity.”', 'Hab. 1:13'], ['The Just Live by Faith', '“the just shall live by his faith.”', 'Hab. 2:4'], ['The Earth Filled', '“the earth shall be filled with the knowledge of the glory of the LORD, as the waters cover the sea.”', 'Hab. 2:14'], ['Silence Before Him', '“the LORD is in his holy temple: let all the earth keep silence before him.”', 'Hab. 2:20'], ['Mercy in Wrath', '“in wrath remember mercy.”', 'Hab. 3:2'], ['Rejoicing Though Empty', '“Yet I will rejoice in the LORD, I will joy in the God of my salvation.”', 'Hab. 3:18'], ['My Strength', '“The LORD God is my strength.”', 'Hab. 3:19']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Habakkuk without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Habakkuk in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Wait for what He has said</b> — Though it tarry, wait (Hab. 2:3).', '<b>Live by faith</b> — The just shall live by his faith (Hab. 2:4).', '<b>Look for His glory</b> — The earth shall be filled with the knowledge of it (Hab. 2:14).', '<b>Be silent before Him</b> — The LORD is in His holy temple (Hab. 2:20).', '<b>Ask mercy in wrath</b> — In wrath remember mercy (Hab. 3:2).', '<b>Rejoice in the LORD</b> — The God of my salvation (Hab. 3:18).', '<b>Find strength in Him</b> — The LORD God is my strength (Hab. 3:19).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Habakkuk', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Habakkuk in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Complaint and the Answer (Ch. 1) — A work in their days. <i>(Hab. 1:5, 13)</i>', 'The Vision and the Woes (Ch. 2) — The just shall live by his faith. <i>(Hab. 2:1–4, 14, 20)</i>', 'The Prayer of Habakkuk (Ch. 3) — Joy in the God of salvation. <i>(Hab. 3:2, 17–19)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Habakkuk, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward faith, patience, and rejoicing in the LORD in hard times without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Habakkuk simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Habakkuk stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]

# Place in God's Storyline (Master Standard v1.155): last item of Section 1
from storyline import S as _STORY
